            <section id="assess-your-lawn">
              <h2>Start with what summer left behind</h2>
              <p>Summer can leave a Lynchburg lawn looking tired: thin grass along a busy walkway, bare spots where the kids played, or weeds that moved into stressed areas. Cooler days are a chance to take stock and plan your next steps. Before buying seed or fertilizer, walk your property and note where the lawn needs attention.</p>
              <p>Check whether problem spots stay wet, dry out quickly, receive heavy foot traffic, or sit in deep shade. Seeding alone will not solve every underlying issue. Tell us what you have noticed when you <a href="/contact">request a quote</a>, so we can discuss services that fit your property and priorities.</p>
              <p>This guide focuses on cool-season lawns such as tall fescue. Bermudagrass and zoysiagrass follow a different seasonal schedule; do not apply a fall fescue renovation plan to them automatically.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="aeration-and-seeding">
              <h2>Give thin turf a fresh start with aeration and seeding</h2>
              <p>For cool-season lawns, late summer into early fall offers a useful opportunity to establish grass before winter. Virginia Cooperative Extension identifies September through mid-October as a favorable seeding period across much of Virginia. Local weather, your grass type, and the time available for establishment should guide the actual date.</p>
              <p>Core aeration removes small plugs of soil and can help relieve compaction. Preparing the soil surface also improves seed-to-soil contact when overseeding thin areas. Choose seed suited to your sunlight and intended use, and make a watering plan before the work begins.</p>
              <p>Our <a href="/services/aeration-seeding">aeration and seeding service</a> combines core aeration, seed selection, and overseeding with guidance for aftercare. Let Edwards Landscape Group handle the preparation and application while you focus on helping the new grass establish. Ask us about timing for your lawn rather than waiting until cold weather arrives.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="mowing-and-watering">
              <h2>Keep mowing, and match watering to the lawn's stage</h2>
              <p>Continue mowing while grass is growing. Use sharp blades, follow the recommended height for your grass, and avoid removing more than one-third of the blade at one time. A sudden low cut is not a shortcut to getting ready for winter.</p>
              <p>Our <a href="/services/mowing">lawn mowing service</a> helps keep that routine consistent as growth changes. If you are planning overseeding, coordinate mowing with the seeding work so young grass has time to establish.</p>
              <p>New seed and established turf have different watering needs. Keep the seedbed consistently moist without puddles during germination, then gradually move toward deeper, less frequent watering as roots develop. Established lawns need adjustments for rainfall and cooler weather. Check the soil instead of leaving a summer irrigation schedule running unchanged.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="feeding-and-weeds">
              <h2>Plan fertilizer and weed control together</h2>
              <p>Fall is an important feeding season for cool-season grass, but more fertilizer is not always better. Use soil testing to guide pH and nutrient decisions, and match the fertilizer amount and timing to the grass and its condition. Do not add lime simply because it is fall.</p>
              <p>If you plan to seed, check weed-control labels first. Some products prevent grass seed from establishing or require a waiting period before or after seeding. A treatment that suits an established lawn may not suit new seedlings.</p>
              <p>Edwards Landscape Group's <a href="/services/fertilization-weed-control">fertilization and weed-control service</a> can help coordinate these jobs. Tell us about any products recently applied and whether you want to overseed, so the proposed plan accounts for both.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="leaves-and-beds">
              <h2>Stay ahead of leaves and tidy the surrounding beds</h2>
              <p>Do not let a thick, wet layer of leaves sit on the grass. Light leaf cover can be finely mulched with a mower when it disperses into the turf; collect heavier accumulations that would leave the lawn covered. Be gentle around newly seeded areas.</p>
              <p>Our <a href="/services/fall-winter-cleanup">fall and winter cleanup service</a> can take leaf removal and seasonal debris off your list. For planting areas, <a href="/services/bed-maintenance">bed maintenance</a> helps address weeds and untidy edges alongside your lawn work. Ask about combining these services in a quote so the lawn and landscape receive attention together.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="fall-checklist">
              <h2>Your summer-to-fall checklist</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Identify your grass type and note thin, shaded, or heavily used areas.</li>
                <li>Plan aeration and overseeding early enough for establishment.</li>
                <li>Arrange watering and mowing around any new seed.</li>
                <li>Coordinate feeding and weed control with your seeding schedule.</li>
                <li>Keep leaves from accumulating and schedule seasonal cleanup.</li>
              </ul>
              <p>You do not have to tackle every job yourself. Whether you need help with one thin section or want mowing, seeding, and cleanup coordinated, Edwards Landscape Group can help you choose a practical starting point.</p>
